  "notes": "This is a scanned, OCR-degraded 5-page document. Pages 1-3 (the Executive Order body) are clearly legible. Pages 4-5 (Appendix D-562-33, the vehicle-application table) are heavily degraded in the scan: many engine family codes are garbled (letters/digits swapped, e.g. 'O' vs '0', 'I'/'1', 'S'/'5'), several vehicle-model cells are missing their leading make-letter (printed as plain '150' rather than 'F150'/'E150'), and a few rows in the 2003 'Additional Vehicles' section have ambiguous cell-to-row alignment. Where a code could not be read with confidence it is flagged inline in trim_note as '(OCR uncertain)'. Model years for the ambiguous rows were cross-checked against the standard VIN model-year code embedded as the first character of each engine family (Y=2000, 1=2001, 2=2002, 3=2003, 4=2004), which is consistent throughout and was used to resolve year assignments with higher confidence than the visible printed year labels alone. The universal front/rear catalytic converter part numbers are 12340 (front) and 12140 (rear) for nearly every row; one row (2000 Expedition/F150, engine family YFMXA04.6HGC) prints the rear number as 12143, and one row (2002 E-350 2WD 6.8L) prints the front number with an apparently dropped leading digit ('2340'); both are flagged rather than silently corrected. Top-level part_numbers also includes 12100/12300 (the series names used in the device title) and 12301/12311 (the series names of the prior exemption this order's application replaces, per the WHEREAS clause), which are naming/series designators rather than table part numbers. Given the OCR quality of the appendix table, a human reviewer should re-verify the engine family codes and the handful of flagged rows directly against the original scan. Executed date read from handwritten '23' on page 3 (page 2's typed placeholder shows a stray '2'); confidence on the exact day is high but reviewer should confirm."
